When the UE enters **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM**, it is in a *combined EPS/non-EPS registration recovery state*:  
- **User data and signalling** for **EPS services** may be sent/received (per Clause 5.1.3.2.4.8);  
- However, **non-EPS (CS domain) services are not yet available**, as indicated by the MM state remaining **MM IDLE**, and the UE must attempt **combined TA/LA updating with IMSI attach** (Cl. 5.5.1.3.4.3 / 5.5.3.3.4.3) to restore CS domain registration;  
- The UE **shall respond to paging for PS domain**, but **may optionally ignore CS-domain paging** (Cl. 5.2.3.2.7, NOTE);  
- Critically, the UE **uses requests for non-EPS services from CM layers** (e.g., MO CS call, emergency CS fallback) to trigger GERAN/UTRAN selection and MM procedures — *not* EPS NAS signalling — reflecting that **signalling for non-EPS services is handled outside EPS NAS**, via legacy MM/GMM.  

Thus, the state reflects **EPS service continuity** alongside **active, NAS-driven recovery of CS domain registration**, with user data/signalling bifurcated: EPS over S1, non-EPS over GERAN/UTRAN via MM.

The UE includes the **EPS bearer context status IE** when it needs to indicate which EPS bearer contexts are active within the UE — specifically:

- Upon **inter-system mobility from WB-S1 or A/Gb/Iu mode to NB-S1 mode** in EMM-IDLE, if it has active dedicated or default EPS bearer contexts that must be locally deactivated and reported (Cl. 6.4.2.1, Cl. 6.5.0, Cl. 6.5.4.2 note);  
- Upon **inter-system change from N1 mode to NB-S1 mode** (single-registration), if it deactivates mapped dedicated EPS bearer contexts (Cl. 6.4.2.1);  
- In the **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST** message after such inter-system changes, to report the updated activation status of EPS bearer contexts (Cl. 6.4.2.1, Cl. 6.5.0);  
- In the **CONTROL PLANE SERVICE REQUEST** message, if the UE wishes to synchronize EPS bearer context status with the network (e.g., to signal inactive bearers for local deactivation by the MME) (Cl. 5.6.1.4.2);  
- As explicitly required by Cl. 8.2.33.4: *“The UE shall include this IE, if it wants to indicate the EPS bearer contexts that are active within the UE.”*

Thus, the inclusion is **triggered by a need to convey the current activation state of EPS bearer contexts to the network**, especially during mobility or service request procedures where bearer context consistency must be maintained.

Upon expiry of timers T3444 or T3445, a UE configured for eCall only mode must perform the **eCall inactivity procedure**, which includes:

- Stopping other running timers (e.g., T3411, T3412);  
- Performing a detach procedure (EPS-only or combined, depending on registration);  
- Deleting EMM context (GUTI, TAI list, last visited registered TAI, equivalent PLMNs, eKSI); and  
- Entering the **EMM-DEREGISTERED.eCALL-INACTIVE** state.

A UE configured for NAS signalling low priority must explicitly set the low priority indicator to **"MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority"** in the following **emergency-related scenarios** (per Clause 4.2A):

1. **Performing an attach for emergency bearer services** (i.e., EPS emergency attach);  
2. **Having an established PDN connection for emergency bearer services** and performing:  
   - EPS mobility management procedures (e.g., tracking area updating), **or**  
   - Establishing a *new* PDN connection for emergency bearer services;  
3. **Performing a service request procedure for a CS fallback emergency call or 1xCS fallback emergency call**;  
4. **Responding to paging** — regardless of domain, but especially when paging relates to emergency service delivery (e.g., MT emergency session setup).  

These exceptions override the default low-priority indication to ensure emergency traffic receives prioritized network treatment and bypasses congestion control mechanisms applied to low-priority NAS signalling.

The UE shall proceed according to **clause 5.5.3.2.6A**, which classifies this as an *abnormal case for SMS services*. Specifically:

- Stop timer T3430 (if running);  
- Increment the tracking area updating attempt counter (unless already = 5);  
- If counter < 5: start timer T3411, set EPS update status to EU1 UPDATED, enter state EMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE; upon T3411 expiry, trigger either the *normal TA update for EPS and "SMS only"* or the *combined TA/LA update* procedure;  
- If counter = 5: start timer T3402, set EPS update status to EU1 UPDATED, enter state EMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE; upon T3402 expiry, trigger the same SMS-involving TA update procedure.

This is explicitly stated in [1] as the defined behavior for “SMS services status IE not treated in clause 5.5.3.2.4A”.

The tracking area updating attempt counter is reset when:

- A normal, periodic, or combined tracking area updating procedure is **successfully completed**;  
- Such a procedure is **rejected with EMM cause #11, #12, #13, #14, #15, #25, or #35**;  
- A **combined attach** or **combined tracking area updating** procedure is completed for **EPS services only** with EMM cause **#2 or #18**;  
- A **new PLMN is selected**;  
- The UE is in substate **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE** or **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM**, and:  
  - a **new tracking area is entered**, or  
  - **timer T3402 expires**, or  
  - **timer T3346 is started**.

*(Per Clause 5.5.3.1 — General, last paragraph)*

The **tracking area updating attempt counter** limits the number of consecutive rejected tracking area update attempts. It is incremented upon a rejected tracking area update and reset when:
- A tracking area updating procedure (normal, periodic, or combined) is successfully completed;
- The procedure is rejected with EMM causes #11, #12, #13, #14, #15, #25, or #35;
- A combined attach or tracking area update is completed for EPS services only with cause #2 or #18;
- A new PLMN is selected; or
- The UE is in substate EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE or EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM, and either a new tracking area is entered, timer T3402 expires, or timer T3346 is started.

The UE **does not need to update forbidden lists** when it receives a **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REJECT in EMM-CONNECTED mode** as a response to a tracking area updating procedure initiated in that mode.

The MME concludes that extended protocol configuration options are supported end-to-end for a PDN connection if:

- The UE is in NB-S1 mode;  
- Or the APN is for UAS services;  
- Or the PDN Type is non-IP or Ethernet;  
- Or the UE has indicated support in the last ATTACH REQUEST or TAU REQUEST, **and** the MME has received the extended protocol configuration options IE in at least one message from the PDN GW for this PDN connection.

The purpose of the dedicated clause for ESM and 5GSM coordination (Clause 6.1.4A, referencing TS 24.501) is to **ensure consistent, interoperable handling of session management procedures across EPS (4G) and 5GS (5G)** — particularly for UEs operating in dual-stack or interworking scenarios (e.g., EPS fallback, inter-RAT mobility, or combined registration). Since ESM (EPS Session Management, TS 24.301) and 5GSM (5G Session Management, TS 24.501) share functional parallels but differ in signaling, state models, and procedure triggers (e.g., PDU Session vs. EPS Bearer context establishment), explicit coordination is required to:

- Prevent conflicting or redundant bearer/session activations/deactivations during inter-system procedures (e.g., handover, TAU with PDN connection reuse, or combined attach/registration);  
- Define clear responsibility boundaries between EMM/ESM and 5GSMM/5GSM when procedures are combined or triggered jointly;  
- Specify how EPS bearer context status (e.g., via EPS Bearer Context Status IE) maps to or interacts with 5GS PDU session states during mobility or mode changes (e.g., WB-S1 → NB-S1 → 5GS);  
- Support backward compatibility and smooth transition mechanisms (e.g., for CIoT optimizations or control-plane data transport) across generations.

This coordination is essential because TS 24.301 governs EPS-only behavior, while TS 24.501 governs 5GS — and their coexistence in deployments mandates normative alignment at the NAS layer.

A UE in **LIMITED-SERVICE** state:

- **Shall perform cell selection/reselection** according to 3GPP TS 36.304;  
- **May respond to paging (with IMSI)** — but this is *optional* (implementation-dependent), e.g., a MUSIM UE may skip paging based on service indication (e.g., voice);  
- **Shall not send user data or non-emergency NAS signalling**, except for:  
  - Service request (as response to paging),  
  - Tracking area updating (if triggered),  
  - Attach for emergency bearer services or RLOS access (per clauses 5.2.3.2.3 and 5.5.1.2.5C);  
- **Shall not initiate normal attach or periodic TAU** unless required for emergency access.

*(Based on Clauses 5.2.3.2.3, 5.2.3.2.5, and 5.2.3.2.7 — all explicitly referencing LIMITED-SERVICE behavior and conditional paging response.)*

When initiating the Remote UE Report procedure, the UE must:  
- Send a **REMOTE UE REPORT** message to the network;  
- Start **timer T3493**;  
- Enter the state **PROCEDURE TRANSACTION PENDING**.  

(Per Clause 6.6.3.2 — Context [2])

The AUTN parameter is defined in TS 24.301 (clause 9.9.3.2) *by reference* to clause 10.5.3.1.1 of TS 24.008 — meaning TS 24.301 does **not** redefine AUTN’s syntax, encoding, or semantics, but delegates those to TS 24.008. However, this reference is **normatively incorrect**, because TS 24.008 (GSM/UMTS GMM/MM) does **not** define AUTN: AUTN is an EPS-specific parameter defined in TS 33.102 and used in EMM procedures per TS 24.301.

Thus, the relationship is a **legacy editorial artifact**: TS 24.301 erroneously cites TS 24.008 instead of the correct specifications (TS 33.102 for format/semantics; TS 33.401 for authentication logic). Implementation must therefore ignore the cited clause in TS 24.008 and instead follow:
- AUTN structure (SQN, AMF, MAC) per TS 33.102 §6.3.3;
- UE validation logic (MAC check, SQN freshness, separation bit) per TS 24.301 §5.4.2.6 and TS 33.401 §6.1.1;
- Error handling (EMM causes #20, #21, #26) per TS 24.301.

In short: the TS 24.008 reference is **non-functional**; implementation relies on TS 33.102/33.401 and TS 24.301 itself — not TS 24.008.

The MME shall return an ATTACH REJECT if the ATTACH REQUEST contains **invalid or unacceptable UE security capabilities**, specifically including:
- All bits zero for EPS encryption algorithms;
- All bits zero for EPS integrity algorithms;
- Lack of support for mandatory EPS encryption algorithms; or  
- Lack of support for mandatory EPS integrity algorithms.

(Per Clause 5.5.1.2.7, item j)

The table listing Information Elements (IEs) for each message definition includes these six specific pieces of information:

1. **Information Element Identifier (IEI)** — in hexadecimal notation (e.g., `B-` for half-octet IEI), if applicable (formats T, TV, TLV, TLV-E);  
2. **Name of the information element** — indicating its semantic purpose (e.g., “Tracking area identity list”);  
3. **Type of the information element** — referencing its coding rule (e.g., “type 4”, or clause 9.9.3.33);  
4. **Presence requirement indication** — `M` (mandatory), `C` (conditional), or `O` (optional), per 3GPP TS 24.007;  
5. **Format of the IE** — one of `T`, `V`, `TV`, `LV`, `TLV`, `LV-E`, or `TLV-E`;  
6. **Length of the IE** — in octets (or permissible range), where `?` signifies that the *maximum length is constrained only by the underlying link layer protocol* (i.e., no NAS-layer-defined upper bound beyond physical/transport limits).

Upon receiving an ATTACH REJECT with EMM cause #42 ("Severe network failure"), the UE shall:

- Set the EPS update status to **EU2 NOT UPDATED**;  
- Delete any stored **GUTI**, **last visited registered TAI**, **TAI list**, **eKSI**, and **list of equivalent PLMNs**;  
- Reset the **attach attempt counter to 5**;  
- Start an **implementation-specific timer** with value = **2 × T**, where *T* is the PLMN selection timer defined in TS 23.122;  
- While this timer runs, **exclude the PLMN + RAT combination** that delivered the reject from PLMN selection candidates;  
- Enter state **EMM-DEREGISTERED.PLMN-SEARCH** and perform **PLMN selection according to TS 23.122**.

Additionally (if supported):  
- In A/Gb or Iu mode: set GMM state to **GMM-DEREGISTERED**, GPRS update status to **GU2 NOT UPDATED**, and delete **P-TMSI**, **P-TMSI signature**, **RAI**, and **GPRS ciphering key sequence number**;  
- In single-registration mode: set **5GMM state = 5GMM-DEREGISTERED**, **5GS update status = 5U2 NOT UPDATED**, and delete **5G-GUTI**, **last visited registered TAI**, **TAI list**, and **ngKSI**.

Upon receiving a SERVICE ACCEPT message that includes the T3448 value IE, the UE must:

1. **Stop** timer T3448 if it is currently running;  
2. **Start** timer T3448 with the value provided in the IE;  
3. **Consider the transport of user data via the control plane as successful**.

*(Per clauses 5.6.1.4.2 — last paragraph — and confirmed by identical logic in ATTACH ACCEPT/TAU ACCEPT handling in clauses 5.5.1.2.4 and 5.5.3.2.4.)*

The Last visited registered TAI IE is omitted when the UE **does not hold a valid last visited registered TAI**, as explicitly mandated by clauses 8.2.4.4 and 8.2.29.8:  
> *"This IE shall be included **if** the UE holds a valid last visited registered TAI."*  

A "valid" last visited registered TAI is defined in TS 24.301 as one that has been successfully stored during a prior successful registration (e.g., after a successful Attach or Tracking Area Update) and has not been deleted due to procedures such as:
- Rejection with EMM cause values that mandate deletion (e.g., #6, #7, #8, #11–#15, #25, #31, #42, #78 — all specify “delete any GUTI, last visited registered TAI, TAI list…”);
- IMSI detach or network-initiated detach with “re-attach not required”;
- Severe failure recovery (e.g., #42 triggers deletion);
- Transition to EMM-DEREGISTERED states where context is purged.

Thus, omission is **not optional** — it is a strict conditional exclusion: no valid value → no IE.
